ai_response:
The procedure for filing an RTI (Right to Information) application is clearly outlined in the RTI Act 2005. As per Section 6(1) of the RTI Act, any citizen of India can file an RTI application with a public authority seeking information that falls under its jurisdiction.

To file an RTI application, one needs to:

* Identify the public authority to which the request is addressed (Section 2(o));
* Specify the information sought in a clear and concise manner (Section 6(1)(a));
* Provide a valid identity proof (Section 8);
* Pay the fees, if applicable, as per the RTI rules of the concerned public authority;
* Submit the application in person or send it through registered post, email, fax, or online mode, as permitted by the public authority.

The time limit for responding to an RTI application is 30 days from the date of receipt of the request (Section 7). However, this period can be extended by another 15 days if the information sought requires processing or collection of data (Section 7(1)).

It's essential to note that the RTI Act requires public authorities to maintain a register for receiving and disposing of RTI applications, as well as disclose information regarding the fees charged and the procedures for filing an application (Section 4).

In conclusion, the procedure for filing an RTI application is straightforward. By following these steps, citizens can effectively exercise their right to access information and hold public authorities accountable.

Source:
RTI Act, 2005 (Act No. 22 of 2005)
 Sections 2(o), 6(1)(a), 7, 8
